<loc>https://docs.memewar.zone/core-concepts/campaigns</loc>
<title>Campaigns</title>
<description>A campaign is a token launch with a built-in trading arena.</description>
<content>In MemeWarzone, a **campaign** is the unit of creation and competition: a token launched by a creator, with a public token page where trading, discovery, and league performance are tracked. A campaign combines: 1. **Token identity** (name, ticker, image, creator) 2. **A market on a bonding curve** (buy/sell against BNB) 3. **Progress toward graduation** (how far the curve has filled) 4. **Discovery** (UpVotes + trending surfaces) 5. **Competition** (Leagues that rank campaigns and traders) ## What you’ll see on a campaign page Typical sections: - **Chart + price**: the bonding curve price changes as people buy/sell. - **Buy/Sell panel**: trade against the curve. - **Curve progress**: how close the campaign is to graduation. - **Activity**: recent trades and volume (where available). - **UpVote**: paid visibility to help a campaign get discovered. - **Leagues**: whether the campaign is currently competing and how it’s doing. ## What the creator controls (and what they don’t) Creators control: - Campaign metadata (branding, links, description) - Their own marketing and community Creators do **not** control: - The market price once trading starts (the curve + trading activity does) - Whether traders can buy/sell (it’s on-chain) ## The campaign lifecycle (high level) 1. **Created**: token + campaign page exists. 2. **Trading on the curve**: price moves as supply changes. 3. **Graduation**: when the campaign hits the graduation target, it transitions to post-graduation liquidity. 4. **After graduation**: trading continues in the broader market; leagues may continue to track performance depending on the season rules. ## What to check before trading - Does the creator identity look consistent (profile + socials)? - Is the campaign early or late on the curve? (late can be more volatile) - Are you sizing the trade for memecoin-level risk? Next: **[Bonding Curve](/core-concepts/bonding-curve)**.</content>
</page>
